No, not without giving you replacement keys. The law says a landlord shall not change the locks during your tenancy unless they give you replacement keys (Residential Tenancies Act, 2006, s. 24).

If a landlord does change the locks without giving you keys, the law allows you to apply to the Board for an order finding that the landlord breached this rule (Residential Tenancies Act, 2006, s. 29(1), para. 5). You must apply within one year of the conduct (s. 29(2)).

If the Board finds this happened, it can:
- order the landlord to pay the reasonable costs of repairing or replacing your damaged or disposed-of property, and other reasonable out-of-pocket expenses (s. 31(1)(b));
- order a rent abatement, meaning a reduction in rent (s. 31(1)(c));
- order an administrative fine paid to the Board, up to the greater of $10,000 and the Small Claims Court monetary limit (s. 31(1)(d));
- order that the landlord not engage in further listed conduct against tenants in the complex (s. 31(1)(a));
- order that the tenancy be terminated (s. 31(1)(e)).

If you were out of the unit and it was vacant when the Board made its decision, the Board may also order that you get back possession and that the landlord not rent the unit to anyone else (s. 31(3)).

A landlord who knowingly changes the locks in a way that breaks section 24 is guilty of an offence (s. 233(b)).

Only the landlord's own changes are covered by these rules. Tenants also cannot change the locks without the landlord's consent (s. 35(1)).

